IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `g1-art2-governor-misconduct`

-- g1-art2-9a-persons.l4: Article 2(9)(a) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 with the PERSONS in it, an additional route beside the caller-supplied relation of g1-art2-governor-misconduct.l4.
-- Source: the consolidated text "from 2 October 2026", INPUTS/charities-jersey-law-2014-2026-10-02.txt; every quotation is a slice of that file (src:N is line N).
-- Status: ASSUMED, NOT RULED. The fleet lead and the general manager decided on 2026-10-27 to add this module; nothing here replaces or changes a rule of g1-art2-governor-misconduct.l4.
--
-- What it adds. g1-art2-governor-misconduct.l4 takes how a person stands to the general control and management as one four-way fact (`Relation to the general control and management`), supplied by the
-- caller, and `Art 2(9)(a)` tests one constructor of it. In that fact "another person" and "that other person" live inside the name of a constructor, and persons are not values. This module keeps
-- the Law's grammar instead: "a person" and "another person" are two separate inputs, introduced on the GIVEN line; "that other person" is the second one used again in the body; "another" is
-- the condition that the other person is NOT the person; and "the entity" is the entity whose constitution it is, so the third input is the constitution of the entity as to the control.
--   "a person"           `the person`, introduced on the GIVEN line
--   "another person"     `the other person`, introduced on the GIVEN line, and the condition that it is not `the person`
--   "that other person"  `the other person`, used again in the body
--   "the entity"         `the constitution of the entity`: who has the control in their own right under it, and who exercises it for whom
-- Then it DERIVES the four-way relation of g1-art2-governor-misconduct.l4 from persons, so that the derived value can be fed to the existing rules `Art 2(9)(a)`, `Art 2(9)(b)` and `Art 2(7)(e)`
-- and to the headline `Art 2(7) the person is a governor of the entity`, which still take the relation as a caller input. The tests assert that the two routes agree.
-- No section GIVEN is used: the three inputs are ordinary rule GIVENs, and no existing rule's GIVEN list is touched.

§ `Part 1 — Interpretation`

§§ `2 Definition of entity and related terms`

§§§ `2(9) general control and management, with the persons`

-- A person. The name alone cannot tell two people of one name apart, so a person also has an identifier; the caller gives distinct persons distinct (name, identifier) pairs.
-- Equality is structural: the same name and the same identifier are the same person; the same name with different identifiers are two persons.
DECLARE Person HAS
    name IS A STRING
        @desc The person's name
    identifier IS A STRING
        @desc Whatever tells two persons of the same name apart (a registry number, a date of birth, an address); persons with the same name and identifier are the same person

-- One person exercising the general control and management of the entity on behalf of someone.
DECLARE `Exercise on behalf of another person` HAS
    `who exercises it` IS A Person
        @desc The person who exercises the general control and management
    `on whose behalf` IS A Person
        @desc The person on whose behalf it is exercised; recorded as the same person when someone exercises it on their own behalf

-- The entity's constitution as to the general control and management of its administration, and the exercises of it on behalf of others that the entity records.
DECLARE `Constitution as to the general control and management` HAS
    `who has it in their own right under the constitution` IS A LIST OF Person
        @desc The persons who, under the constitution of the entity, have the general control and management in their own right, alone or as one of a number
    `who exercises it on behalf of whom` IS A LIST OF `Exercise on behalf of another person`
        @desc Each exercise of the general control and management on behalf of someone, as the entity records it

-- src:103 | (a) a person is not a governor merely by virtue of exercising general control and management on behalf of another person who has the general control and management in that other person’s own right under the constitution of the entity; and
-- This rule is the CONDITION the paragraph describes, not the conclusion: the person exercises it on behalf of another person who has it in that other person's own right.
-- "Another" is NOT (the other person EQUALS the person); a person recorded as exercising it on their own behalf is not caught.
-- "Who has the general control and management in that other person's own right under the constitution of the entity" is membership of the list the constitution gives.
@ref Article 2(9)(a) (src:103)
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the other person` IS A Person
      `the constitution of the entity` IS A `Constitution as to the general control and management`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 2(9)(a) the person exercises the general control and management on behalf of another person who has it in that other person's own right` `the person` `the other person` `the constitution of the entity` MEANS
        NOT (`the person` EQUALS `the other person`)
    AND any (GIVEN exercise YIELD     exercise's `who exercises it` EQUALS `the person`
                                  AND exercise's `on whose behalf` EQUALS `the other person`)
            (`the constitution of the entity`'s `who exercises it on behalf of whom`)
    AND elem `the other person` (`the constitution of the entity`'s `who has it in their own right under the constitution`)

-- The derivation. Which of the four ways of standing to the control a person has, from the persons:
--   * the person has it in their own right: `holds ... alone` if they are the only person who does (counting a person listed twice once), `holds ... as one of a number of governors` if there are several (9)(b);
--   * otherwise, if (a) holds for some other person: the person only exercises it on behalf of another who has it in that other's own right;
--   * otherwise the person holds none of it.
-- "Merely" in (a) is read as "only": a person who has the control in their own right AND exercises it for another is not one who merely exercises it for another, so is not the third constructor;
-- the first branch comes first for that reason. The other person ranges over the persons who have it in their own right, because (a) requires the other person to be one of them.
-- src:102 | (9) For the purpose of paragraph (7)(e) –
-- src:104 | (b) a person is a governor whether that person has that general control and management alone or as one of a number of governors.
@ref Article 2(9)(a) and (b) (src:102-104)
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the constitution of the entity` IS A `Constitution as to the general control and management`
GIVETH A `Relation to the general control and management`
`Art 2(9) the relation of the person under the constitution` `the person` `the constitution of the entity` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF elem `the person` (`the constitution of the entity`'s `who has it in their own right under the constitution`)
        THEN IF count (nub (`the constitution of the entity`'s `who has it in their own right under the constitution`)) EQUALS 1
             THEN `holds the general control and management alone`
             ELSE `holds the general control and management as one of a number of governors`
        IF any (GIVEN `the other person` YIELD
                    `Art 2(9)(a) the person exercises the general control and management on behalf of another person who has it in that other person's own right` `the person` `the other person` `the constitution of the entity`)
                (`the constitution of the entity`'s `who has it in their own right under the constitution`)
        THEN `exercises the general control and management only on behalf of another who holds it in that other's own right`
        OTHERWISE `holds none of the general control and management`
